Z||000000||superXmotorsports||11-22-1999||09:53 PM||||Well I ordered my clutch today.  Since I couldn't really get many Fiero owners to confess what clutch they use  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I bought the RAM powergrip Kevlar clutch.  I couldn't get a response back from FOCOA on their Kevlar clutch $232 vs $600 (est FOCOA clutch).  I'll let you know what happens the 1st time down the 1/4 with 300+ ponies flyin!||||reg||3
Z||000001||fiero56||11-22-1999||10:03 PM||||I'd really like to hear how it stands up.  I got the regular organic clutch material with two more springs for better grip but I haven't even split the case yet. I still wonder if I made the right descision.||||reg||7
Z||000002||theogre||11-22-1999||11:41 PM||||56, didn't you say your extra spings where in the disk? Those springs don't provide more gripe. They provide better shock absorbtion durring engagement. This makes the disk last longer but not any improvment in gripe. It will just gripe like new for longer.<P>Now it the springs are in the pressure plate.... those would help clamping power.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||11-23-1999||08:50 AM||||On my calls for clutches I got all sorts of different answers on what extra springs in the disk do. Guess I had some brain clout last night.  Ogre, you are exactly right, again.||||reg||14
Z||000004||2birds||11-24-1999||04:54 PM||||I used a Ram clutch in my 86 GT.  The guy at Ram told me to ask for P/N 88644, and to have Summit ship it directly from the factory.  If I recall correctly (this was in 1996) the Ram guy said he would put in a 6 spring vs. 4 spring disc.  He told me they used this in the kits some of the Fiero specialty stores sold.  I still only paid $140 for the kit.  Its been 31,000 miles, and I thrash it pretty hard all the time.  The pressure plate is the LUK type, same as the Pontiac dealer used 5 years earlier.  The tech's name was John, and they were really easy to get help from. ||||reg||1
